WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:05.363
Mr. Gorbachev, tear down this wall.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:09.300
Major changes are now taking place

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.577
in the Soviet Union.

00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:13.720
The Baltic peoples of Estonia, Latvia

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:17.290
and Lithuania will be free to
determine their own future.

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:19.210
Mingling with the rush hour traffic,

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:21.440
Red Army armored personnel carriers

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.920
on the streets of Moscow this morning.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:25.040
Momentous events today.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.500
The course of history is changed.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.870
Mikhail Gorbachev resigned.

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:32.180
The Soviet Union collapsed 30 years ago

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:35.710
in part because its government-run
economy was incapable

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:38.630
of producing blue jeans,
cigarettes, and automobiles

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:40.480
that its citizens wanted.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:41.860
What it was great at,

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.990
was producing champion chess players.

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:46.420
From the end of World War Two,

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:49.750
until the evil empire dissolved in 1991

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:51.410
all but one world champion,

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:55.390
the American Bobby Fisher,
represented the USSR.

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:57.740
None was better than Gary Kasparov,

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:00.560
who became the world champion in 1985

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.780
at the record, young age of 22.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:05.850
Widely considered the
greatest chess player

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:07.010
in modern history,

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:09.870
he held that title for 15 years.

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:11.110
As a chess prodigy,

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:13.240
he traveled abroad for competitions

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:16.210
and he describes youthful
trips to France and Germany,

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.710
as nothing short of revelatory.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:20.690
The casual abundance of what used

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:21.790
to be called the Free World,

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:23.780
just felt different, he says.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:25.096
Beyond the iron curtain,

00:01:25.096 --> 00:01:28.260
he encountered the anti-communist
works of George Orwell

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.450
and was able to read exile dissident,

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.660
Alexander Solzhenitsyn's
suppressed indictments

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.370
of totalitarianism.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:36.530
As the eighties progressed,

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:39.120
he publicly questioned the Soviet system.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.110
In 1990,

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.350
he joined the Democratic Party of Russia

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:45.950
and became increasingly outspoken
in favor of human rights,

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:49.330
representative democracy
and limited government.

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:50.800
In post-Soviet Russia,

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.840
he used this celebrity and
influence to spearhead attempts

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.990
to build civil society and
conduct fair elections,

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:58.700
emerging as a leading critic

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.960
of Russian leader, Vladimir Putin.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:02.920
By the early 2010s,

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:04.810
he had been arrested for participating

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:07.800
in unauthorized
anti-government demonstrations

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.800
and was widely believed to be the author

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:13.860
of a popular petition
demanding Putin's resignation.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:15.840
As chairman of the
Human Rights Foundation,

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.120
Kasparov continues to lobby for freedom

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.030
in the former Soviet Union and beyond.

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:22.050
In September,

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:24.900
Reason spoke with the chess
Grandmaster in New York

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.970
about what it was like
to be the beneficiary

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:29.866
of a catastrophically
failed Soviet system.

00:02:29.866 --> 00:02:32.160
And what lessons the world,

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.870
especially American democratic
socialists should remember,

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:38.620
three decades after its collapse.

